e each range.' This means for each medium, the secret is uniform in their range. It does NOT mean the ranges are independent conditional on the secret. It means the secret is fixed, and the ranges are drawn such that they contain the secret. So if I know my range is [2,41], the secret is uniform in [2,41]. If I also know Eileen's range is [3,42], does that change my probability distribution? Yes. The fact that Eileen's range is [3,42] and contains the secret means the secret is in [3,42]. So the secret is in [2,41] ∩ [3,42] = [3,41]. Is it uniform in [3,41]? The problem says 'The secret is equally likely to be at any position inside each range.' This phrasing is slightly ambiguous. Does it mean P(S=s | R_me) = 1/40 for s in R_me? And P(S=s | R_eileen) = 1/40 for s in R_eileen? If so, and if the ranges are independent conditional on S, then P(R_me, R_eileen | S) = P(R_me|S)P(R_eileen|S). Then P(S | R_me, R_eileen) is proportional to P(R_me|S)P(R_eileen|S)P(S). If P(S) is uniform on 1-100, and P(R|S) is constant for all R containing S (since the range is just a window of 40 containing S), then the likelihood is constant for all S in the intersection. So yes, the secret is uniform in the intersection of all stated ranges. Intersection: [2,41] ∩ [3,42] ∩ [12,51] ∩ [40,79] = [40,41]. So S is 40 or 41. I will guess 40.",
